"[Innovation](https://www.forbes.com/innovation/)[AI](https://www.forbes.com/ai/)\n\n# These 10 Chinese Robotics 
Startups Are Winning The Most VC Funding\n\nBy[Edith Yeung](https://www.forbes.com/sites/edithyeung/ 
\"https://www.forbes.com/sites/edithyeung/\"),\n\nContributor.\n\nForbes contributors publish independent expert 
analyses and insights.\n\n Early-stage venture investor, tech executive and creator of the Crane.\n\n--:-- / 
--:--\n\nThis voice experience is generated by AI. [Learn 
more](https://www.forbes.com/sites/forbesdigitalgroup/article/forbes-ai-audio-learn-more/).\n\nThis voice experience is 
generated by AI. [Learn 
more](https://www.forbes.com/sites/forbesdigitalgroup/article/forbes-ai-audio-learn-more/).\n\nRobotics is one of the 
most significant funding trends of 2026, and China is where all the action is. Here are now more than 150 humanoid 
robotics companies in the country, and that number continues to grow, according to China’s National Development and 
Reform Commission.\n\nI am currently in China to explore these developments firsthand. It has been fascinating to visit 
companies such as Unitree and Geely, and I look forward to sharing more of my findings with readers. Through my 
research, I have identified the 10 Chinese humanoid startups that have raised the most funding.\n\n## Seven Trends I 
Learned About Chinese Humanoid Startups\n\nDuring my travels, I have picked up on seven key trends in the Chinese 
humanoid startup space.\n\n### 1. Humanoid Startup Funding is Accelerating Rapidly\n\nHumanoid startup funding is 
growing rapidly in China, with top companies condensing years of growth into just months. For example, Galbot and 
Agibot, both founded in 2023, have already raised a combined total of nearly $2 billion. In contrast, it took Unitree, 
founded in 2016, over 10 years to raise $246 million.\n\n### 2. Big Valuation Gap Between Chinese and Silicon Valley 
Humanoid Startups\n\nChinese humanoid valuations currently appear to lag significantly behind their U.S. counterparts. 
For example, Figure AI recently raised a $1 billion Series C at a $39 billion valuation despite not yet having launched 
a product. In contrast, Unitree is valued at only [$7 billion for its 
IPO](https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/chinese-robotics-firm-unitree-eyeing-7-billion-ipo-valuation-
sources-say-2025-09-08/), even aftershipping 5,500 humanoid robots in 2025.\n\nMORE FOR YOU\n\n### 3. Shenzhen is not 
the only Mecca for Humanoid Robots\n\nWhile Shenzhen is often regarded as the \"Silicon Valley of China,\" this trip has
highlighted that it is not the only epicenter for robotics. Hangzhou, Shanghai and Beijing have also emerged as major 
hubs for humanoid startups.\n\n### 4. The Best Robotics Talents Come from DJI, Huawei Microsoft, Tsinghua and Zhejiang 
University\n\nWhile the top robotics engineers in Silicon Valley typically emerge from Tesla, Apple, MIT or Stanford, 
the leading founders in China are coming from Huawei, DJI, Microsoft, Tsinghua University, Zhejiang University, Harbin 
Institute of Technology and Huazhong University of Science and Technology.\n\n### 5. Embodied Intelligence over Simple 
Robotics\n\nThese startups are not just building hardware; they are integrating AI software into physical devices like 
robots and smart cars. This \"embodied intelligence\" allows them to interact with the world through physical bodies and
sensors.\n\n### 6. Active Funding Participation from Corporations and Government Funds\n\nThe most active investors are 
not typical Silicon Valley venture firms. Instead, the landscape is dominated by large platform companies — including 
Tencent, Alibaba, Meituan, ByteDance and Baidu — alongside state-owned government funds and corporate venture arms from 
the energy, automotive and manufacturing sectors.\n\n### 7. Shanghai and Hong Kong Are Go-To IPO Destinations for 
Robotics Startups\n\nThe Shanghai Stock Exchange (STAR Market) and the Hong Kong Stock Exchange (HKEX) are currently the
most active markets for the sector for exit. Two sign…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
