raised $325 million in two new funds and added partners Camille Ricketts and Art Clarke from Notion and Apple. When Cobi
Blumenfeld-Gantz, CEO of Medicare-focused startup Chapter, showed his end-of-year numbers to Ross Fubini in late 2023, 
the tech investor quickly presented him with an offer: let his firm, XYZ Venture Capital, kick off his next fundraise. 
Now, with XYZ’s fourth early-stage fund, a $200 million vehicle, and a $125 million second opportunity fund in the bank,
XYZ’s doubling its partnership to four with the hires of Camille Ricketts, a veteran of work collaboration software 
unicorn Notion, and Art Clarke, until now a leader of AI infrastructure at Apple. For its new funds, its partnership has
doubled to include Ricketts, who worked with Hamilton at First Round and then led Notion’s marketing efforts before a 
stint as an operating partner at another venture firm; and Clarke, Fubini’s former colleague and longtime running buddy 
who spent three years leading product at Palantir at his introduction, and who until recently held an important role in 
Apple’s AI and machine learning efforts.", "score": 0.35133386, "raw_content": 
"[Innovation](https://www.forbes.com/innovation/)[Venture Capital](https://www.forbes.com/venture-capital/)\n\n[Editors'
Pick](https://www.forbes.com/editors-picks/)\n\n# XYZ Invested In Anduril And 15 Other Palantir Alumni Startups. Now 
It’s Branching Out.\n\nBy[Alex Konrad](https://www.forbes.com/sites/AlexKonrad/ 
\"https://www.forbes.com/sites/AlexKonrad/\"),\n\nFormer Staff.\n\nSenior editor covering venture capital and 
startups\n\nUpdated Jul 30, 2024, 11:28am EDT\n\nXYZ partners Ross Fubini, Chauncey Hamilton, Art Clarke and Camille 
Ricketts with CFO Steve Hilk, and staffers JeanClaude Dorsainvil, Lucy Wilmot, Angie Kirk, Christine Hubbard, Mackenzie 
Bohannon, Alyssa Kelley and Alexa Romanoff.\n\nXYZ Venture Capital\n\n### Midas Seed staple Ross Fubini and his firm 
have raised $325 million in two new funds and added partners Camille Ricketts and Art Clarke from Notion and 
Apple.\n\n---\n\nWhen Cobi Blumenfeld-Gantz, CEO of Medicare-focused startup Chapter, showed his end-of-year numbers to 
Ross Fubini in late 2023, the tech investor quickly presented him with an offer: let his firm, XYZ Venture Capital, kick
off his next fundraise.\n\nThe entrepreneur had first met Fubini in 2020 through the alumni network for Palantir, the 
Peter Thiel cofounded software provider for governments and big business that now trades at a market cap of about $60 
billion. Back then, Fubini was known for early checks into founders from that community, such as Anduril, the defense 
tech firm 
[reportedly](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-06-26/anduril-s-1-5-billion-deal-underscores-vc-zeal-for-defen
se-tech 
\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-06-26/anduril-s-1-5-billion-deal-underscores-vc-zeal-for-defense-tech\") 
set to be valued at $14 billion. XYZ was small, investing out of its debut fund, and with its second partner, Chauncey 
Hamilton, barely started.\n\nBut after fighting his way onto the cap table just after Chapter’s seed round that year, 
Fubini proved his value quickly. Two of Chapter’s three biggest partner institutions signed up after his introductions. 
And when Chapter raised a Series A from Thiel and JD Vance’s Narya Capital, it was Fubini who hosted Blumenfeld-Getz at 
his house, helping craft its winning pitch deck.\n\nSo when XYZ — now bigger, with a growing team and hundreds of 
millions in assets — offered to pour more money in, it wasn’t a difficult choice. XYZ led Chapter’s $50 million round in
May at a valuation of more than $500 million, according to startup data tracker PitchBook. “Ross doesn’t follow the 
prevailing winds of VC or the zeitgeist,” Blumenfeld-Gantz said. “He doesn’t view himself as being above doing the hard 
grunt work.”\n\nThe ability to go deeper with a select few companies — doubling, even tripling down — was a big reason 
Fubini struck out on his own nearly a decade ago; now, with XYZ scaling up, it’s a vision turning increasingly into 
reality.\n\nMostly solo investing in the Palantir network propelled [Fubini](https://www.forbes.com/profile/ross-fubini/
\"https://www.forbes.com/profile/ross-fubini/\"), 48, onto all three annual editions of the *Forbes* [Midas Seed 
List](https://www.forbes.com/lists/midas-seed/ \"https://www.forbes.com/lists/midas-seed/\") of the world’s top 
earliest-stage investors. Now, with XYZ’s fourth early-stage fund, a $200 million vehicle, and a $125 million second 
opportunity fund in the bank, XYZ’s doubling its partnership to four with the hires of Camille Ricketts, a veteran of 
work collaboration software unicorn Notion, and Art Clarke, until now a leader of AI infrastructure at Apple.\n\nAnduril
remains the firm’s biggest winner so far — XYZ’s investment is now worth tens of millions at Anduril’s most recent 
valuation, enough paper gains to return its first fund several times over — though XYZ can boast no major exits yet. But
with $760 million in assets now under management and its new team, XYZ now hopes to become known for more than its 
public sector and ex-Palantir track record, and for moving aggr…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
"https://www.bloomberg.com/features/2024-uk-startups-to-watch", "title": "25 UK Startups to Watch in 2024: Wayve, Flo, 
Sibstar", "content": "Venture capital investments, which had stagnated in 2023, returned to growth by the midpoint of 
the year and the AI mania unleashed by OpenAI two years ago was apparent in the UK. Wayve, a London-based autonomous 
