| Series A ($75M, Aug 2023) | Google Ventures, Capital Factory | ~$500M |\n| Unitree Robotics | ~$200M | Series B+ 
(2024) | Shunwei Capital, Sequoia China | ~$1B |\n| Booster Robotics | ~$50M | Series A (2024) | Strategic investors | 
~$200M |\n| Agility Robotics | ~$600M | Series B ($150M, 2024) | Amazon Industrial Innovation Fund | $1B+ |\n| Tesla 
(Optimus) | Internal | N/A | Self-funded | N/A |\n| Sanctuary AI | ~$150M | Series C ($95M, 2024) | InBC, Evok 
Innovations | ~$500M |\n| Physical Intelligence | ~$400M | Seed ($70M) + Series A ($400M) | Jeff Bezos, Thiel, Khosla, 
Sequoia | $2B |\n\nThe total exceeds $12B when including smaller companies, corporate R&D programs (Hyundai/Boston 
Dynamics, Honda, Toyota Research), and Chinese competitors (UBTECH, Fourier Intelligence, Galbot, Agibot). For context, 
the entire industrial robot market was ~$16B in annual revenue in 2023. The venture capital invested in humanoids in 3 
years rivals the annual revenue of the entire established robotics industry.\n\n**What this means for buyers:** Heavy 
investment means rapid improvement in capability, declining prices, and strong support ecosystems. It also means some 
companies will fail, and their robots will become unsupported. Buying from well-funded companies with clear paths to 
