"https://www.forbes.com/sites/kenrickcai/2023/10/11/character-ai-chatbots-group-chat", "title": "Character.AI Wants To 
Add Its Popular Chatbots Into Your Group Chat", "content": "# Character.AI’s $200 Million Bet That Chatbots Are The 
Future Of Entertainment. Noam Shazeer, CEO and cofounder of Character AI. ## In its latest product launch, the unicorn 
AI startup is bringing bots that make it sound like you’re talking to well known people like Taylor Swift into your 
group chat. But rather than coding, many of them are engrossed in lively group chats with their colleagues and the AI 
chatbot characters that Character has become known for. The group chat feature is Character.AI’s latest move in 
cofounder and CEO Noam Shazeer’s bet that people will want to interact with a variety of different chatbot personas, 
rather than having a single, all-encompassing AI companion. By allowing the company’s subscribers, who pay $9.99 per 
month, to interact with each other and some of the startup’s 18 million AI bots in chats at the same time, Character is 
hoping to establish dominance as the go-to app for AI-powered entertainment while adding to its immediate revenue 
stream.", "score": 0.07777658, "raw_content": "[Innovation](https://www.forbes.com/innovation/)[Venture 
Capital](https://www.forbes.com/venture-capital/)\n\n![Character AI-Noam Shazeer-Cody Pickens for 
Forbes-1x1](https://imageio.forbes.com/specials-images/imageserve/65257fc3ee9169a4354c9497/Character-AI-Noam-Shazeer-Cod
y-Pickens-for-Forbes-1x1/0x0.jpg?format=jpg&height=1080&width=1080)\n\n[Daily 
Cover](https://www.forbes.com/daily-cover-stories/)\n\n# Character.AI’s $200 Million Bet That Chatbots Are The Future Of
Entertainment\n\nNoam Shazeer, CEO and cofounder of Character AI.\n\nCody Pickens for Forbes\n\nUpdated Oct 12, 2023, 
05:42pm EDT\n\n## In its latest product launch, the unicorn AI startup is bringing bots that make it sound like you’re 
talking to well known people like Taylor Swift into your group chat.\n\n#### By [Kenrick 
Cai](https://www.forbes.com/sites/kenrickcai/ \"https://www.forbes.com/sites/kenrickcai/\"), Forbes 
Staff\n\n---\n\nInside Character.AI’s disheveled Palo Alto, California headquarters, employees at first appear to be 
hard at work, glued to their computer monitors. But rather than coding, many of them are engrossed in lively group chats
with their colleagues and the AI chatbot characters that Character has become known for. Now, thanks to a new group chat
function the startup launched Wednesday, they were chatting with work friends along with bots that anyone can build to 
create the illusion that you’re actually talking to the likes of Napoleon Bonaparte, Tony Stark or Lucifer.\n\n“The 
feature was good enough that people stopped working sometimes to use it,” cofounder Daniel De Freitas told *Forbes*. De 
Freitas, the company’s president, has been particularly partial to gathering team members together to play Ship AI, a 
chatbot programmed to act like a text-based adventure game that lets group chat participants pretend to be astronauts 
exploring outer space.\n\nThe group chat feature is Character.AI’s latest move in cofounder and CEO Noam Shazeer’s bet 
that people will want to interact with a variety of different chatbot personas, rather than having a single, 
all-encompassing AI companion. By allowing the company’s subscribers, who pay $9.99 per month, to interact with each 
other and some of the startup’s 18 million AI bots in chats at the same time, Character is hoping to establish dominance
as the go-to app for AI-powered entertainment while adding to its immediate revenue stream. Shazeer said that after 
“some period of time,” the company plans to make the feature free “because our goal is to empower everyone with AGI,” or
artificial general intelligence.\n\nBacked by nearly $200 million from VCs including Andreessen Horowitz, and valued at 
$1 billion in a March funding round, Character.AI has wowed the startup community with its engagement time — it claims 
registered users who send at least one message on a given day spend an average of two hours on the platform. Since its 
initial launch in September 2022, 20 million users have signed up for Character.AI, though traffic to its website has 
been declining since its May 2023 peak, per analytics provider SimilarWeb. Character.AI spokesperson Rosa Kim said after
publication that total traffic, which accounts for mobile app usage, has increased in that span.\n\nAlso in May, the 
startup began monetizing through its subscription service, which gives customers priority access to its platform 
(servers have often been overloaded, and non-paying users have to wait longer to get on) and early access to new 
features. Character.AI declined to share any statistics on its subscribers or its recent growth traction.\n\nThe group 
chat feature, which *Forbes* tested out, is still rudimentary. In the initial iteration of Character.AI’s group chat, 
human users are able to chat with one another as on any messaging platform. To invoke a bot’s response, however, a user 
must manually tap on the icon for the specific bot they want to speak, or a dice icon which selects one of the bots in 
the group chat to respond at random. The bots can respond to us…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
