multiple public investors assign it.", "raw_content": "# Agility Robotics SPAC gives humanoids their first public-market
test\n\n## The merger values the robot maker at $2.5B and could set the tone for Figure, 1X and Unitree.\n\n*Ali 
Javaheri is a senior research analyst at PitchBook covering emerging spaces*\n\n[Agility 
Robotics](https://my.pitchbook.com/profile/171265-96/company/profile) is taking humanoid robotics public, and the deal 
is arriving at a useful moment for both the category and the SPAC market.\n\nThe Oregon-based maker of Digit, a humanoid
robot for industrial use, agreed to merge with [Churchill Capital Corp 
XI](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/232663-42), the Michael Klein-backed SPAC, in a deal valuing the company at 
$2.5 billion and expected to provide more than $620 million in proceeds. The combined company is expected to trade as 
AGLT, giving public investors their first US pure-play humanoid robotics comp.\n\nThat is the part that matters. 
Humanoids have mostly traded in private markets around demos, AI narratives and long-dated labor substitution. Agility 
is pitching something more concrete. [Amazon](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/104872-06) was an early investor 
and has run multiple pilots, while Digit has been deployed with GXO, Toyota Motor Manufacturing Canada, Schaeffler and 
Mercado Libre. At GXO, Digit cleared more than 100,000 totes, or bins used in automation settings, under a multiyear 
robotics-as-a-service contract, one of the clearest commercial deployment signals in the humanoid category 
globally.\n\nThe SPAC wrapper is still the overhang. Our data shows [123 SPACs were raised in 
2025](https://pitchbook.brightspotcdn.com/90/a2/ce59db7f46c084a521e7b678691a/q2-2026-pitchbook-analyst-note-spacs-at-vcs
-gate.pdf), and more than 50 closed in Q1 2026 as the venture market searches for liquidity. But the last SPAC cycle 
performed poorly for VC-backed companies, and SEC changes have made it harder to sell aggressive revenue projections 
around limited operating traction. That raises the bar for Agility.\n\nThe [robotics 
market](https://pitchbook.com/news/reports/q1-2026-robotics-physical-ai-vc-trends) is moving in the same direction. Q1 
2026 was the strongest quarter on record, with $16.3 billion invested across 492 deals. The diligence filter has also 
shifted from “does the technology work?” to “can it scale, and does it have paying customers?”\n\nAgility is now the 
first public-market test of that question for humanoids, with Figure, 1X and Unitree likely to be repriced against 
whatever multiple public investors assign it.\n\nLearn more about our [editorial 
standards](https://pitchbook.com/news/pitchbook-news-editorial-standards-code-conduct)."}, {"url": 
