"https://pitchbook.com/news/articles/comcast-split-could-drive-deals-though-not-necessarily-for-pe", "title": "Comcast 
split could drive deals, though not necessarily for PE - PitchBook", "score": 0.019361824, "published_date": "Mon, 29 
Jun 2026 22:15:58 GMT", "content": "## The largest deals in the media sector have been dominated by corporate buyers, 
with PE firms preferring to play on the fringes, PitchBook data shows. Comcast’s plan to spin off its media and theme 
park business, announced on Monday, revived speculation that legacy media could be entering another wave of dealmaking. 
Strategic buyers dominate the sector’s biggest deals, PitchBook data shows. Some of the largest such deals occurred in 
2025, PitchBook data shows. One exception to PE’s “picks and shovels” approach to investing in the media sector was the 
$8 billion merger of Paramount and Skydance, also in 2025. The dominance of strategic investors is clear in the numbers 
for 2026, which at first glance looks like a banner year for the US media and adtech industries. By segment, movies, 
music and entertainment accounts for about $112.3 billion of 2026 deal value across just 33 deals, while media and 
information services accounts for 248 deals totaling $20.2 billion.", "raw_content": "Limited Partners\n\n# Comcast 
split could drive deals, though not necessarily for PE\n\n## The largest deals in the media sector have been dominated 
by corporate buyers, with PE firms preferring to play on the fringes, PitchBook data shows.\n\nComcast’s plan to spin 
off its media and theme park business, announced on Monday, revived speculation that legacy media could be entering 
another wave of dealmaking.\n\nUnder the plan, [NBCUniversal](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/10461-25) and 
[Sky](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/42001-93) will separate from Comcast’s broadband and wireless business, 
giving existing shareholders shares in both listed entities.\n\nThe move follows Comcast’s January spinoff of Versant 
Media Group, which houses cable networks such as CNBC, MS NOW, USA Network, E! and Golf Channel.\n\nThe sector has 
reason to consolidate. Cord-cutting keeps hollowing out the cable bundle, the cost of competing in streaming continues 
to climb, and debt-laden conglomerates are under pressure to simplify sprawling structures.\n\nInvestors reacted 
favorably to today’s news, sending Comcast’s share price up around 4.5% during the day’s trading.\n\nThe data, however, 
suggests that even if the deal floodgates open, PE firms are likely to be highly selective.\n\nStrategic buyers dominate
the sector’s biggest deals, PitchBook data shows. Alongside the [Paramount 
Skydance](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/10610-65)-[Warner Bros. 
Discovery](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/25348-15) merger finalized this year, the all-time top three also 
include [Disney](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/10612-18)’s $71.3 billion acquisition of film studio [21st 
Century Fox](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/57961-99) and WBD’s $42.4 billion merger with 
[WarnerMedia](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/11634-76), in 2019 and 2022, respectively.\n\nPrivate equity’s 
media and adtech bets have largely been focused on smaller, cash-generative assets tied to data, marketing services, 
distribution and media infrastructure, rather than the biggest Hollywood content libraries. Some of the largest such 
deals occurred in 2025, PitchBook data shows.\n\nThat year, [Silver 
Lake](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/investor/10278-19) closed its $13 billion take-private of 
[Endeavor](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/53004-70), now WME Group, betting on talent representation, sports and
live entertainment assets, while TPG acquired [DirecTV](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/10817-65) in a $7.6 
billion transaction that completed [AT&T](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/10141-57)’s retreat from pay TV.\n\nOne
exception to PE’s “picks and shovels” approach to investing in the media sector was the $8 billion merger of Paramount 
and Skydance, also in 2025. PE firms [KKR](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/investor/10066-15) and [RedBird Capital 
Partners](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/investor/110680-12) backed the deal, the latter putting up $2 billion.\n\nThe 
dominance of strategic investors is clear in the numbers for 2026, which at first glance looks like a banner year for 
the US media and adtech industries. M&A in the sector has already reached $134.2 billion across 333 deals through June 
23, eclipsing all full-year figures since 2018, when deal value reached $135.6 billion.\n\nYet the headline number is 
mostly made up of a single transaction: Paramount Skydance’s $110 billion acquisition of Warner Bros. Discovery. 
Excluding that deal, 2026 YTD media and adtech M&A value falls to roughly $24.2 billion.\n\nThe median US media deal 
this year is just $2.3 million, against an average near $3.98 billion—a couple of strategic mega-deals and a long tail 
of small ones.\n\nBy segment, movies, music and entertainment accounts for about $112.3 billion of 2026 deal value 
across just 33 deals, while media and information services accounts for 248 deals totaling $20.2 billion.\n\nAnalysts 
were quick to float NBCUniversal as a future takeover target, even as Comcast…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
